                    Emil Mana
                </a>

                                        <font size="-2">
            [
                <font color="#336699">22/06/26</font>

                            ]
            </font>

        </td></tr>
        <tr id="comment-body-91004"><td bgcolor="#ffffff">
            <p><a href="https://ongage.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3A178ab9eb-31e9-404b-b08c-7abb38554137" class="user-hover" rel="557058:178ab9eb-31e9-404b-b08c-7abb38554137" data-account-id="557058:178ab9eb-31e9-404b-b08c-7abb38554137" accountid="557058:178ab9eb-31e9-404b-b08c-7abb38554137" rel="noreferrer">Ofer Sapir</a> </p>

<p>I asked claude to take a look and here is the answer:</p>

<div class="preformatted panel" style="border-width: 1px;"><div class="preformattedContent panelContent">
<pre>The actual conflict

  The mailing was built with a domain-split distribution (explicit gmail.com per-connection quotas), but the account has domain splitting turned off. These two settings are mutually
  incompatible — per-domain quotas only function when domain_group is allowed to reflect the real recipient domain.

  Fix options:
  1. Set disable_domain_split to 0/null on account 1025168 so domain_group reflects the real domain and the gmail bucket is honored separately (this is the direct fix for this account).
  2. Or, at the point the distribution_config is generated, don't emit per-domain (gmail.com) quota entries when the account has disable_domain_split=1, since they can never be satisfied —
  and surface a validation warning instead of silently mis-sending.

  Option 1 is what restores the 5000+3000 behavior you expect.

  Want me to look at where disable_domain_split is set / where the distribution_config is generated to scope a code-level fix for option </pre>
</div></div>

<hr />

<p>looking for what claude fixing for option 2</p>
        </td></tr>
